**Read:** [Multizone airflow and contaminants — Dols and Polidoro, CONTAM 3.4](../sources/CONTAM-Multizone-Airflow-and-Contaminants-NIST.lingua#r_5HECDC480RSYAJ3SQ2QNFHCN46), airflow paths and chapter 8.3; revisit the earlier pressure-reference Record.

A room pressure is meaningful relative to a specified point and elevation. A flow path responds to the pressure difference along it, which may include fan, hydrostatic and wind terms. A sensor connected to an unsuitable reference can report a stable number while another boundary reverses.

For an isothermal constant-density teaching model, Q=Cq sign(Δp)|Δp|ⁿ, where Cq has units m³ s⁻¹ Pa⁻ⁿ. A mass-flow coefficient has different units and may need density corrections. Empirical behavior near zero pressure and changing flow regimes needs explicit treatment.

**Work:** Use hypothetical Cq=0.01 m³ s⁻¹ Pa⁻½ to compare Δp=4, 0 and −4 Pa. Draw the reference sides of a pressure measurement. Explain how fitting a coefficient at one state differs from establishing its validity over temperature, opening and flow changes.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
